   465	## 5. Exact completed-local quartic controls
   466	
   467	The following controls prove that none of the preceding phenomena is a
   468	formal-local contradiction.  They do **not** assert that the local algebras
   469	glue to the charged global `S_4` cover or arise from a global Keller map.
   470	
   471	### 5.1 Collision away from `B`
   472	
   473	Let `R=C[[u,v]]` and
   474	
   475	```text
   476	S=R^4,
   477	tau=(u^mu,v^mu,1,2),
   478	P(Z)=(Z-u^mu)(Z-v^mu)(Z-1)(Z-2).                       (5.1)
   479	```
   480	
   481	Then `S` is a rank-four finite-etale `R`-algebra and is the normalization of
   482	the generically monogenic order `R[tau]`.  Near the origin,
   483	
   484	```text
   485	Disc(P)=unit*(u^mu-v^mu)^2.                             (5.2)
   486	```
   487	
   488	Take the local target curve `C_0` to have equation `uv=0`.  On the first
   489	sheet designate `u=0` as the local branch of `Phi`; on the second designate
   490	`v=0`.  The other branch on each sheet is residual.  Then
   491	
   492	```text
   493	(uv)^mu/u^mu=v^mu,       (uv)^mu/v^mu=u^mu,            (5.3)
   494	```
   495	
   496	which is exactly the inverse-Kummer identity on the selected sheets.  The
   497	normalization is etale and has trivial canonical module, while the
   498	monogenic order has precisely the square index divisor (5.2).  Thus a
   499	same-fibre node and a nonzero conductor coexist with quartic etaleness and
   500	`K=0`.
