   122	
   123	Every reduced ruling fibre is one `A1`; `Phi` itself is `A1`.  The branch is
   124	connected but is **not initially assumed irreducible**.  Put
   125	`r=#Irr(B)>=1`.  Every irreducible component is generically `(2,1,1)`; the
   126	whole connected curve has one `(3,1)` cusp value `c`, one `(2,2)` two-point
   127	value `n`, and `beta1(B)=1`.  The companion fibre cardinalities are `2,1,0`
   128	respectively.  Section 4 will derive `r=1`; no use before that point may
   129	silently assume it.
   130	
   131	The cyclic pseudo-cover theorem used below is Miyanishi Lemma 2.5.2, already
   132	source-checked and different-model-reviewed in the cubic funnel.  Chau's
   133	primary Theorem 4.4 and the Orevkov degree formula quoted in Remark 4.9 are
   134	read directly from *Non-zero constant Jacobian polynomial maps of C2*,
   135	Ann. Polon. Math. 71 (1999), 287--310, DOI
   136	`10.4064/ap-71-3-287-310`.  No classification of arbitrary pseudo-planes,
   137	no typed `(d,n,r)` canonical formula, and no unreviewed Jacobian claim is
   138	imported.
   139	
   140	## 2. Canonical cyclic replacement of the first leg
   141	
   142	Apply Miyanishi Lemma 2.5.2 to `(U,rho)`.  Base-change the ruling by the
   143	degree-`mu` cyclic map
   144	
   145	```text
   146	x |-> a+c*x^mu
   147	```
   148	
   149	and normalize.  This gives a finite etale cyclic cover
   150	
   151	```text
   152	f_tilde:U_tilde->U,             deg(f_tilde)=mu.         (2.1)
   153	```
   154	
   155	The inverse image of `Phi` is a disjoint union of `mu` affine lines
   156	
   157	```text
   158	L_0 disjoint_union ... disjoint_union L_(mu-1).
   159	```
   160	
   161	Each `L_j->Phi` has degree one: their degrees sum to `mu`, the cyclic group
   162	acts transitively, and a connected finite etale cover of `A1` is trivial.
   163	Delete `L_1,...,L_(mu-1)`.  Miyanishi's lemma identifies the resulting open
   164	with `A2` and makes the restriction
   165	
   166	```text
   167	f_mu:A2->U
   168	```
   169	
   170	surjective, etale, and of generic degree `mu`.
   171	
   172	The base-change coordinate `x` restricts to a polynomial on this `A2`.
   173	For `x!=0` its fibre is the base change of an ordinary ruling fibre; for
   174	`x=0` it is the retained line `L_0`.  All scheme fibres are reduced `A1`s.
   175	Equivalently this is a smooth `A1`-bundle over `A1`, hence trivial.  After an
   176	affine change of source and base coordinates, `x` is a coordinate and
   177	
   178	```text
   179	rho o f_mu=a+c*x^mu.                                    (2.2)
   180	```
   181	
   182	Now put `H=pi o f_mu`.  It is a morphism `A2->A2`, hence a polynomial pair.
   183	Both factors are etale on their displayed domains, so `det JH` is a nowhere-
   184	zero polynomial and therefore a nonzero constant.  The field tower and
   185	surjectivity give
   186	
   187	```text
   188	deg_geo(H)=4mu,                 H(A2)=pi(U)=A2-{n}.      (2.3)
   189	```
   190	
   191	Thus the conditional rank-four survivor canonically manufactures another
   192	Keller counterexample of degree `4mu`.
   193	
   194	For the original first leg, `mu|d1` and `mu>=2`.  If the original map has
   195	minimal geometric degree among all plane Keller counterexamples, (2.3)
   196	cannot have smaller degree than `4d1`.  Hence `mu>=d1`; divisibility gives
   197	`mu=d1`, proving (0.2).  This is a minimal-counterexample reduction, not an
   198	identity for an arbitrary nonminimal factorization.
   199	
   200	If a canonical-normalization first leg `d1=1` satisfied this same pseudo-plane
   201	package, (1.1) would say `mu|1`, immediately contradicting `mu>=2`.  The
   202	cyclic replacement is needed only in the strict-block case `d1>=2`; no claim
   203	is made about canonical rank-four configurations outside this package.
   204	
   205	## 3. The deleted fibre forces a second singular asymptotic curve
   206	
   207	Let `Ubar` be the normalization of `Y` in `C(U_tilde)`.  Excellence makes
   208	`Ubar->Y` finite of degree `mu`; over `U` it restricts to (2.1).  The open
   209	source `A2` has boundary in `Ubar` consisting exactly of
   210	
   211	```text
   212	the inverse image of R=Y-U,       and       L_1,...,L_(mu-1). (3.1)
   213	```
   214	
   215	The finite extension of `H` maps the first part to `B` and every deleted
   216	line to
   217	
   218	```text
   219	C0=pi(Phi)=H(L_0).                                      (3.2)
   220	```
   221	
   222	Therefore the nonproper-value set of `H` is exactly `B union C0` set-
   223	theoretically.  At this stage `C0` could still coincide with one irreducible
   224	component of a reducible `B`; this possibility is not discarded by notation.
   225	
   226	The map `H|L_0:A1->A2` is polynomial and nonconstant.  Any polynomial map
   227	from `A1` to an affine curve is finite onto its image: a nonconstant
   228	coordinate polynomial makes `C[t]` integral over the image ring.  Hence
   229	`C0` is closed.  Since `L_0 subset A2` and `H(A2)=A2-{n}`, one has
   230	`n notin C0`.  But `n in B`, so `C0` is not the whole connected curve `B`.
   231	After Section 4 proves `B` irreducible, this sharpens to
   232	
   233	```text
   234	C0!=B.                                                   (3.3)
   235	```
   236	
   237	The full map `pi o f_tilde` is etale along every `L_j`; in particular every
   238	restriction `L_j->C0` is everywhere immersive.  Also each deleted line is a
   239	dicritical boundary line for `H`.  Chau Theorem 4.4 applies to its polynomial
   240	image even if another dicritical has the same image: `C0` must be singular.
   241	The exact degree and type of that singularity will follow from saturation in
   242	Section 4.
   243	
   244	## 4. Orevkov--Chau degree budget: irreducibility and saturation
   245	
   246	Apply the degree-at-infinity formula quoted by Chau in Remark 4.9 to the
   247	Keller map `H`.  On a regular extension it has the form
   248	
   249	```text
   250	deg(H)-1 = sum_L [m_L + sum_p(deg_p(Hbar)-m_L)],         (4.1)
   251	```
   252	
   253	where `L` runs over dicritical boundary lines, `m_L` is the generic local
   254	degree, and every displayed correction is nonnegative.  Blowing up the
   255	finite normalization in Section 3 only refines these contributions.
   256	
   257	For clarity, a standard normalization fact translates generic missing rank
   258	into a lower bound in (4.1).  If a dicritical line maps with degree `s` to
   259	the normalization of its image curve and has generic transverse degree `m`,
   260	then its bracket is at least `ms`: the finite ramification of the polynomial
   261	map `A1->A1` contributes `m(s-1)` in addition to the initial `m`.  Summing
   262	over all lines above one image component is therefore at least the generic
   263	missing multiplicity along that component.  Self-identifying two distinct
   264	normalization points creates no derivative correction.
   265	
   266	Let `r=#Irr(B)`.  At a generic `(2,1,1)` value of every component, the
   267	missing ramified rank of `pi` is two.  After the cyclic degree-`mu` cover,
   268	the generic multiplicity of the part of (3.1) lying over `R` is `2mu` on
   269	each component.  Equivalently, at its generic DVR the valuation identity
   270	`sum_j e_j f_j=mu` for `Ubar->Y`, multiplied by the rank-two local factor of
   271	`pi`, gives `sum_j 2e_j f_j=2mu`.  Thus these ramification-boundary curves
   272	contribute at least `2mu*r`.
   273	
   274	At the unique `(3,1)` cusp the missing local rank is three rather than two.
   275	The cusp is unibranch, so this increase cannot be supplied by a second
   276	normalization preimage (unlike the node).  After the cyclic cover it therefore
   277	supplies an additional `mu` in the local-degree corrections; any nonflat
   278	specialization in the finite normalization only increases it.  Consequently
   279	the total ramification-boundary collection over `B` contributes at least
   280	
   281	```text
   282	2mu*r+mu.                                               (4.2)
   283	```
   284	
   285	The `(2,2)` node does not force another correction: it has two normalization
   286	preimages, each carrying the generic rank-two packet.  This is the same
   287	reason a nodal self-identification is invisible to the derivative of its
   288	normalization parametrization.
   289	
   290	Next consider `C0`.  There are exactly `mu-1` deleted lines.  Along each,
   291	the extended map is etale and its image is nonconstant.  Each bracket is at
   292	least one, even if `C0` initially coincides with a component of `B`.  Their
   293	total is at least
   294	
   295	```text
   296	mu-1.                                                    (4.3)
   297	```
   298	
   299	Equations (4.2)--(4.3) and `deg(H)=4mu` give
   300	
   301	```text
   302	4mu-1 >= (2mu*r+mu)+(mu-1)=2mu*r+2mu-1.
   303	```
   304	
   305	Since `r>=1`, this forces
   306	
   307	```text
   308	r=1,                                                     (4.4)
   309	```
   310	
   311	and every inequality is an equality.  This is the promised proof that the
   312	connected target branch is irreducible.  Now `n notin C0` proves
   313	`C0!=B`, so `C0` is a genuine second irreducible component of the nonproper-
   314	value set.
   315	
   316	Equality in (4.3) also says that every deleted line contributes exactly one.
   317	Factor `L_j=A1->C0` through the normalization.  That normalization is `A1`:
   318	it is a normal rational affine curve admitting a finite surjection from
   319	`A1`, and it cannot have a puncture because a nonconstant unit would pull
   320	back to a nonconstant unit of `C[t]`.  Contribution one forces the finite
   321	lift `A1->A1` to have degree one.  Since the ambient map is etale along
   322	`L_j`, the normalization parametrization is immersive.  Hence
   323	
   324	```text
   325	L_j=A1 -> C0 is the immersive normalization map.        (4.5)
   326	```
   327	
   328	Chau Theorem 4.4 forces `C0` to be singular.  Equation (4.5) excludes a cusp
   329	or any other unibranch critical parametrization, so its singularity identifies
   330	at least two distinct normalization points.  Transport through
   331	`L_0 isomorphic to Phi` proves
   332	
   333	```text
   334	there exist p!=p' in Phi with pi(p)=pi(p').              (4.6)
   335	```
   336	
   337	The collision value cannot be `n`, which has no point in `U`, or `c`, whose
   338	companion fibre in `U` has only one point.  It lies either outside `B`, where
   339	two of the four etale points of a `pi`-fibre lie on `Phi`, or at an ordinary
   340	`(2,1,1)` value of `B`, where both companions lie on `Phi`.  The data do not
   341	choose between these alternatives.  The collision gives `C0` an incidence
   342	cycle but does not add to the ruling defect `Q`.
   343	
   344	Finally, equality leaves no spare degree-at-infinity budget for another
   345	unibranch/critical boundary event.  The cusp consumes the sole positive jump
   346	over `B`; the node consumes none; and the singularity of `C0` is the
   347	budget-free immersive self-identification in (4.6).  This is a consistency
   348	theorem, not an exclusion.
   349	
   350	It also identifies a precise possible closing move.  Any independent theorem
   351	forcing a critical parametrization on `C0`, an additional positive local-
   352	degree jump above `B`, or a third dicritical contribution would make (4.1)
   353	strict and close the horn.  Mere self-intersection or extra tangency between
   354	distinct immersed branches does not.
   355	
   356	## 5. Exact Euler and orbit ledger for `q=b o H`
   357	
   358	Let
   359	
   360	```text
   361	D=V(q)=f_mu^(-1)(T),       q=b o H.
   362	```
   363	
   364	Because `H` is etale and `b` is reduced, `D` is reduced.  Sections 3--4 give
   365	`C0!=B`, so `Phi` is not a component of `T`; set
   366	
   367	```text
   368	A=T intersect Phi,            N=#A<infinity.             (5.1)
   369	```
   370	
   371	The full finite-etale pullback `T_tilde=f_tilde^(-1)(T)` has
   372	
   373	```text
   374	e(T_tilde)=mu*e(T)=-3mu.
   375	```
   376	
   377	Every deleted `L_j` meets `T_tilde` in the copy of `A` lying over `Phi`.
   378	Deleting `mu-1` disjoint copies gives
   379	
   380	```text
   381	e(D)=-3mu-(mu-1)N,             D intersect L_0=A,        (5.2)
   382	```
   383	
   384	which is (0.5).  In particular
   385	
   386	```text
   387	e(D)=N mod mu.                                           (5.3)
   388	```
   389	
   390	No component of `T` can be a ruling fibre.  If an `A1` ruling fibre were a
   391	component, its polynomial image under the finite `pi` would be a closed
   392	irreducible curve dense in the now irreducible `B`, hence all of `B`.  It
   393	would have to contain `n`, contradicting `n notin pi(U)`.  Thus the
   394	projection
   395	
   396	```text
   397	x:D->A1
   398	```
   399	
   400	has finite fibres.  Let `nu` be its generic cardinality.  Every special
   401	cardinality is at most `nu`: the finite Zariski-Main completion of each curve
   402	component is torsion-free, hence flat over the smooth affine line, while `D`
   403	is obtained by deleting points.  Away from `x=0`, all cyclic opens coincide
   404	and the deck group sends `x` to `zeta*x`; it preserves `D`.  Hence the
   405	nonzero exceptional values occur in free orbits of size `mu`, with a constant
   406	defect on each orbit.
   407	
   408	Constructible Euler integration for a quasi-finite curve map gives
   409	
   410	```text
   411	e(D)=nu-sum_s(nu-#D_s).
   412	```
   413	
   414	The zero-fibre defect is `nu-N`.  Choose one representative from each
   415	nonzero exceptional orbit and call the sum of its defects `Delta`.  Combining
   416	with (5.2),
   417	
   418	```text
   419	-3mu-(mu-1)N = nu-(nu-N)-mu*Delta,
   420	Delta=N+3.                                               (5.4)
   421	```
   422	
   423	This proves (0.6).  It also shows exactly why the tempting conclusion
   424	`mu|e(D)` is false: the `mu-1` deleted copies of `A` contribute the residue
   425	`N`.  The only valid divisibility statement is (5.3).
   426	
   427	There is an exact cusp dichotomy.  Let `u_c` be the unique point of `T` over
   428	the `(3,1)` cusp value.
   429	
   430	```text
   431	u_c notin Phi:   D has mu cusp points in one nonzero deck orbit;
   432	u_c in Phi:      D has one retained cusp at x=0 and mu-1 deleted ones.
   433	```
   434	
   435	The node `n` has no point of `D` in either case.  Formula (5.4) forces at
   436	least one nonzero exceptional companion-incidence orbit, but this may be a
   437	tangency, a collision, or a loss at infinity of `T->A1`.  It says nothing
   438	about reducibility or multiplicity of the ambient ruling fibre, all of which
   439	remain reduced affine lines.
   440	
   441	The `Pic` and canonical data are fully consumed but add no hidden numerical
   442	term.  The exact order `mu` of `[Phi]` is what selects the cyclic cover and,
   443	for the original leg, proves `mu|d1`.  Also
   444	
   445	```text
   446	f_mu^*K_U=K_A2~0.
   447	```
   448	
   449	Since `T` is principal, adjunction gives `omega_T~O_T`.  This agrees exactly
   450	with etale pullback from the plane hypersurface `B`, whose dualizing sheaf is
   451	also trivial.  Thus a Riemann--Hurwitz or canonical-class correction cannot
   452	be inserted into (5.4), and `K_U~0` alone imposes no congruence on `N`.
   453	
   454	## 6. Sharp polynomial-fibre firewall
   455	
   456	The Euler mechanism in Section 5 has a uniform exact control.  Put
   457	
   458	```text
   459	G(t)=(t-1)^3(t-2)(t-3)(t-4)(t-5),
   460	Q(t,y)=y^2-G(t),
   461	q_mu(x,y)=Q(x^mu,y).                                    (6.1)
   462	```
   463	
   464	The curve `Q=0` has one ordinary cusp at `(1,0)` and no node.  Its
   465	normalization is
   466	
   467	```text
   468	z^2=(t-1)(t-2)(t-3)(t-4)(t-5),        z=y/(t-1),
   469	```
   470	
   471	a genus-two projective hyperelliptic curve with one point removed.  The cusp
   472	normalization is point-bijective, so
   473	
   474	```text
   475	e(Q=0)=2-2*2-1=-3.                                      (6.2)
   476	```
   477	
   478	At `t=0` the curve has two points, so `N=2`.  The pullback `q_mu=0` has
   479	exactly `mu` ordinary cusps, one over each root of `x^mu=1`, and no nodes.
   480	Projection to `x` has generic cardinality two and exactly `5mu` deficient
   481	fibres, one over every root of `x^mu in {1,2,3,4,5}`.  Hence
   482	
   483	```text
   484	e(q_mu=0)=2-5mu
   485	             =-3mu-(mu-1)*2,
   486	sum_nonzero_orbit_reps(2-#fibre)=5=N+3.                 (6.3)
   487	```
   488	
   489	Meanwhile every fibre of the ambient coordinate ruling `x:A2->A1` is a
   490	reduced irreducible `A1`.  Thus a coordinate power, base Euler number `-3`,
   491	one cusp orbit, absence of a source node, and the exact divisibility/orbit
   492	ledger do not force a second bad ruling fibre.
   493	
   494	This family is deliberately only a **polynomial-fibre firewall**.  It does
   495	not realize a Keller pair `H`, a quartic finite-flat algebra, the target node
   496	with companion counts `2/1/0`, the pseudo-plane `Pic` and canonical package,
   497	or a counterexample to JC2.  It blocks only arguments that forget those
   498	additional structures.
   499	
   500	## 7. Maximum-safe conclusion and next gate
   501	
   502	Promote exactly the following conditional statement.
   503	
   504	> **`R4-CYCLE-1-FUNCTION-PAIR` cyclic normalization.**  Every strict-block
   505	> survivor in the charged one-cusp rank-four row canonically produces a
   506	> degree-`4mu` plane Keller counterexample `H=pi o f_mu` with image
   507	> `A2-{n}` and coordinate-power first function `rho o f_mu=a+c*x^mu`.
   508	> A minimal-degree survivor has `mu=d1`.  The Orevkov--Chau budget forces
   509	> `B` irreducible.  The nonproper-value set then has a second component
   510	> `C0=pi(Phi)!=B`; `C0` is normalized immersively by the retained line and
   511	> must have a multibranch self-identification.  For `q=b o H`, equations
   512	> (5.2)--(5.4) give the exact Euler and cyclic-orbit deficit.  The infinity
   513	> budget is saturated by `B` and the `mu-1` deleted lines.
   514	
   515	Do not promote an exclusion of `R4-CYCLE-1`, a second bad ruling fibre,
   516	`mu|3`, `N=0`, `C0 intersect B!=empty`, finiteness of either pseudo-cover,
   517	or any claim that the node/cusp determines the gluing of the companion
   518	divisor.
   519	
   520	The cheapest successor is now narrower than the original function-pair
   521	brief:
   522	
   523	1. determine whether the quartic algebra or `K_U=0` forces the immersed
   524	   self-collision of `C0` to lie on `B`; if so both generic companions lie on
   525	   the single fibre `Phi`, an exact divisor-intersection configuration;
   526	2. calculate the based infinity relation for the two components `B` and
   527	   `C0`, using the saturated budget to reject any forced critical jump;
   528	3. use the exact finite number `N=#(T intersect Phi)` and cusp alternative in
   529	   (5.4), rather than the false divisibility `mu|e(T)`.
   530	
   531	No replay is attached: the new claims are theorem-level normalization,
   532	Euler additivity, and symbolic all-`mu` identities.  Section 6 is proved
   533	uniformly by its displayed normalization and fibre count; sampling finitely
   534	many `mu` values would not strengthen it.
   535	
   536	Nothing here treats `R4-CYCLE-0`, disconnected branch, second-leg degree at
   537	least five, the primitive/no-block horn, constructs an unconditional Keller
   538	counterexample, or proves/disproves JC2.
